Ze-Reviewed-Against: github.com/opencontainers/runc v1.5.3

H-1 of the runc series: newInitProcess starts the Go init directly, with no C
stage (libcontainer/nsenter) in front of it. Ze builds with cgo off, so the C
stage that upstream runs first, and that creates the namespaces from the
bootstrap data, does not exist in a ze binary.

The hunk sets the clone flags of the init command from the configured
namespaces, and a new session, which the C stage made with setsid(2). It
creates the container cgroup with no process attached, opens its directory,
and has the standard library clone init into it (CLONE_INTO_CGROUP): init must
be in its cgroup before its cgroup namespace exists, or that namespace is
rooted at the daemon's cgroup. Upstream attaches init after the start and lets
the C stage unshare the cgroup namespace afterwards. It builds no bootstrap
data, because nothing reads it.

What only the C stage could do is refused with an error, never ignored: a
namespace with a path, time offsets, identity mappings, and a parent death
signal. The standard library makes a child with a death signal compare its
parent pid, which reads 0 inside a new pid namespace, and kill itself.

The descriptor of the cgroup directory rides in Process.clonedExes, which
Container.start closes once init is spawned.

Needs runc-04-cgroup-v2-manager.patch at run time: Apply(-1) creating the
cgroup with no process is the cgroup v2 manager's behavior.

No upstream pull request is opened for this change: upstream keeps its C
stage, and the change is specific to a build with cgo off. The design is
docs/architecture/container/runtime.md.

Delete this series when Ze no longer vendors the runtime.

